WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:03.343
In this scene from "Mission:
Impossible -- Fallout,"

00:00:03.343 --> 00:00:06.930
Ethan Hunt jumps 25,000 feet

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.110
out of a plane before landing in Paris.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.240
A risky move like this

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.400
would normally require a stunt double,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.010
but that's actually Tom Cruise,

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.660
and that background behind him

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.420
is not a green screen.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.520
The actor is famous

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.060
for almost always doing his own stunts,

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.770
no matter how dangerous.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.940
From climbing the world's tallest building

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.250
to hanging off the side of a plane

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.230
to pulling off perhaps

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:31.658
one of the most dangerous
helicopter chases

00:00:31.658 --> 00:00:33.410
ever captured on film,

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.690
Cruise is always finding
new ways to top himself.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.024
And he's not stopping anytime soon.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.015
At more than 2,700 feet,

00:00:46.015 --> 00:00:47.470
the Burj Khalifa in Dubai

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.550
is the world's tallest building.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.380
So it was only a matter of time

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.760
before Cruise decided to climb it.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.593
For this stunt,

00:00:54.593 --> 00:00:57.420
the actor had to climb
1,700 feet in the air,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.318
so he had to wear a special harness

00:00:59.318 --> 00:01:01.860
attached to strategic
points in the building.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.118
To do this, the crew needed

00:01:03.118 --> 00:01:05.993
to break 26 different windows --

00:01:05.993 --> 00:01:08.190
with permission, of course.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.930
This harness was so tight,

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.430
Cruise said it cut off his circulation.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.700
The 65-millimeter IMAX cameras

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.690
and additional helicopter
shooting the scene

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.420
only had a limited
amount of time to record,

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.120
so they had to move fast.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.810
But Cruise wasn't just
climbing the building.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.010
He also had to fall four stories

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.330
and run down the surface of the building

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:31.230
in a move known as an Australian rappel.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.522
All of this required a lot of preparation.

00:01:33.522 --> 00:01:35.160
Cruise practiced by climbing up

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.770
a makeshift glass wall

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.635
heated with artificial lights

00:01:38.635 --> 00:01:40.250
to replicate the hot temperature

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.890
of the windows on the Burj Khalifa.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.630
Stunt coordinator Gregg Smrz

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.420
estimated that Cruise and the crew

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.220
put in at least 200
hours of rehearsal time.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.350
This isn't Cruise's first
experience climbing, however.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.720
In 2000's "Mission: Impossible 2,"

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.950
he climbed a 2,000-foot cliff in Utah

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.628
attached to nothing but a thin safety rope

00:02:01.628 --> 00:02:03.380
and had to jump 15 feet

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:05.547
from one cliff to another.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.390
The stunts in "Edge of
Tomorrow" were tough,

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:14.960
but it was actually the costume

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.910
that made it even more so.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.526
Throughout the film, Cruise's character

00:02:18.526 --> 00:02:21.130
wears metal exoskeleton armor,

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:24.000
something that typically
might be added on through CGI.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.890
But Cruise wore a real suit,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:29.150
sometimes for up to six
hours a day straight.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.330
One version weighed 85 pounds,

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.440
and another version
weighed around 130 pounds,

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.810
thanks to a sniper rifle

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.650
and missile launcher on the back.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:38.872
Here's what Cruise normally

00:02:38.872 --> 00:02:41.150
looks like running in a scene.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.828
Compare that to this shot of him

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:44.705
and his costar Emily Blunt

00:02:44.705 --> 00:02:47.240
running while both wore the suits.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.590
In another stunt for the film,

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.288
the actor was attached to a wire

00:02:50.288 --> 00:02:53.370
and thrown across the
room while wearing it.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:54.610
To take some of the weight off

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.440
and help them run more easily,

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.280
Cruise and Blunt were
often attached to cables.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.805
It took Cruise 30 minutes
to get into the suit

00:03:01.805 --> 00:03:03.530
and 30 to get out.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.379
The team eventually got that down

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:07.360
to around 30 seconds.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.897
While Cruise had plenty of experience

00:03:13.897 --> 00:03:15.990
flying planes in "Top Gun,"

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.673
in "Rogue Nation," he
decided to go into the air

00:03:18.673 --> 00:03:22.860
in a rather unconventional
and more terrifying way.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.390
For the film's opening scene,

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:28.232
he had to cling onto the
side of an Airbus A400M

00:03:28.232 --> 00:03:30.245
that took him up 1,000 feet

00:03:30.245 --> 00:03:31.939
at a speed of 100 knots

00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:33.770
for six to eight minutes.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.000
The plane took off,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.530
did a complete circuit, and landed --

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.663
all with Tom Cruise secured to the side

00:03:39.663 --> 00:03:41.463
with this wire that would later be erased

00:03:41.463 --> 00:03:43.913
in postproduction.

00:03:43.913 --> 00:03:46.590
Because the plane went
so high into the air,

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.750
Cruise had to wear special contacts

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.770
to protect his eyes from flying debris

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.750
and strong gusts of wind.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.690
Plus, the crew had to make sure

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.140
takeoff conditions were absolutely clear.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.310
If they ran into even a single bird

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.340
or some rocks on the runway,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.860
it could severely injure the actor.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.310
The stunt had to be
performed over and over again

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:09.170
until the actor, director, and crew

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:10.590
felt they got it right.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.911
For Cruise, he didn't
feel confident in the take

00:04:12.911 --> 00:04:15.303
until he had gone up eight times.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.680
The fifth "Mission"
entry contained not one

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.040
but two death-defying stunts,

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.520
this one involving
military-style preparation.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:29.739
In the film, Ethan Hunt

00:04:29.739 --> 00:04:31.970
needs to open an underwater vault.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.740
The vault itself was created using CGI,

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:35.770
but Cruise still needed

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.560
to work underwater for the sequence,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.630
which clocks in at just
about six minutes long.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.797
He started by jumping off a 120-foot ledge

00:04:43.797 --> 00:04:46.928
into a tank filled 20
feet high with water.

00:04:46.928 --> 00:04:49.073
Then Cruise needed to film takes

00:04:49.073 --> 00:04:50.450
while holding his breath

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.724
for four to six minutes.

00:04:52.724 --> 00:04:53.665
According to Cruise,

00:04:53.665 --> 00:04:55.910
most underwater sequences
might have an actor

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:58.310
hold their breath for up to 10 seconds,

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.180
so this was definitely a challenge.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.070
He trained with free-diving
expert Kirk Krack

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.404
on a breath-hold special
operations program

00:05:06.404 --> 00:05:08.700
designed for military personnel.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.220
Part of his training
involved breathing exercises

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.410
that would lower his standard heart rate,

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:16.900
essentially training his
body to use less oxygen.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.033
And he wouldn't just be sitting

00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:19.600
in the water during the shoot --

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.440
he would be swimming around.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:22.623
Why so long?

00:05:22.623 --> 00:05:25.450
Well, they had just 10
days to film the sequence,

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.490
so director Christopher McQuarrie

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.670
shot in a series of continuous takes,

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.770
meaning the actor couldn't
easily pop up for air.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.630
While Cruise hasn't
gone to space -- yet --

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.700
he has performed a zero-gravity stunt.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.010
In "The Mummy," he had to hold

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.210
his breath underwater yet
again, dodge explosions,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.500
and even fight Russell Crowe.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.663
The biggest standout
stunt was this sequence,

00:05:51.663 --> 00:05:54.605
in which a cargo plane
plummets to the ground.

00:05:54.605 --> 00:05:56.477
Cruise was offered the chance

00:05:56.477 --> 00:05:58.180
to shoot it on a soundstage,

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.680
but he declined and
opted for the real thing.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.580
To imitate the experience of the crash

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.120
where the characters
float up into the air,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.820
they shot on a plane that NASA

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.310
typically uses to train astronauts.

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:12.960
They decked out the inside
of the plane with padding

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.400
to make it look like a real cargo plane.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.430
The plane couldn't go
into zero-gravity mode

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:20.490
until it was at about 25,000 feet.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:21.510
Once at that position,

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.340
it would free-fall for 22 seconds.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:25.173
During that time,

00:06:25.173 --> 00:06:27.860
Cruise endured four high-altitude flights.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.600
The sequence was eventually wrapped

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.000
after a whopping 64 takes.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.260
And while Cruise's stunts

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.700
usually require a lot of rehearsal,

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:37.770
the actor actually said

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.580
that that was quite tough here,

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.172
as floating in the air
makes things unpredictable.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.090
Cruise has flown a plane

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:51.090
and hung onto the side of one,

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.110
but for "Fallout,"

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.490
he also learned to fly a helicopter

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:57.120
as part of an aerial chase sequence.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:58.690
The 2018 film contains

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:01.000
an array of the actor's
most daring stunts,

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:02.460
like this motorcycle chase,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.018
in which the actor rode
through the streets of Paris,

00:07:05.018 --> 00:07:07.590
sometimes at 100 miles per hour.

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:08.900
For the helicopter chase,

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.500
Ethan Hunt flies through
dangerous mountain terrain

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:13.360
to stop a nuclear bomb.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.888
Before shooting, Cruise
worked 16 hours a day

00:07:16.888 --> 00:07:20.746
to hit the required 2,000
hours of helicopter training.

00:07:20.746 --> 00:07:22.591
There would be up to 13 helicopters

00:07:22.591 --> 00:07:24.491
close to each other in the sky at once,

00:07:24.491 --> 00:07:27.221
so Cruise and the stunt
team carefully planned out

00:07:27.221 --> 00:07:28.790
every little move in advance

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:30.992
using these toy helicopters.

00:07:30.992 --> 00:07:33.634
And the actor needed all the
preparation he could get,

00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:35.640
as he would be acting, piloting,

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.420
and operating the cameras

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.648
mounted to the front of his chopper

00:07:39.648 --> 00:07:41.270
all at the same time.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:42.940
All that planning was crucial,

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:47.134
particularly for this
360-degree corkscrew dive,

00:07:47.134 --> 00:07:48.599
a move that's challenging

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:50.960
even for professional pilots.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.165
According to Matt Evans,

00:07:52.165 --> 00:07:54.490
an instructor at the school
where Cruise trained,

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:56.960
the stunt involves
starting with a descent,

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:58.515
rolling into a turn,

00:07:58.515 --> 00:08:01.050
and then holding the turn as you go down.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:02.240
While that's happening,

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.731
the actor can never take
his hands off the controls.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:13.110
In "Mission: Impossible 2,"

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.130
Cruise's character jumps
out of a helicopter,

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.950
but that scene was shot
using a green screen.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:20.440
In "Fallout," Cruise decided

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.550
to actually jump out of a plane

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:24.320
and became the first actor to do

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.080
a HALO jump on camera.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.370
HALO stands for high
altitude, low opening.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:32.510
He jumped from a height
of over 25,000 feet

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.530
but didn't open his parachute

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.160
until he was below 2,000 feet.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:39.300
This military move allows a soldier

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:41.580
to jump into battle undetected.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.800
The actor practiced in a giant wind tunnel

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.180
constructed on the set,

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.960
and then, according to stunt
coordinator Wade Eastwood,

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.583
jumped out of a plane around 100 times.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.466
And a trained stunt-camera operator

00:08:54.466 --> 00:08:56.420
also had to jump out of
the plane with Cruise

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:57.810
to get the shot.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.765
To make sure every facial expression

00:08:59.765 --> 00:09:01.720
was still visible on camera,

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.992
the crew developed a
special helmet for Cruise

00:09:03.992 --> 00:09:05.020
with a light in it,

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.912
allowing him to breathe
properly while falling.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:14.760
In the long-awaited sequel

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.470
to Tom Cruise's 1986 breakout film,

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:20.520
Cruise is back in the
cockpit of a fighter plane.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.940
The film was shot on real
military aircraft carriers,

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.600
and Cruise flew in real planes.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.354
A new camera system allowed the crew

00:09:28.354 --> 00:09:32.330
to put six IMAX-quality
cameras inside the cockpit.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:33.690
In the original "Top Gun,"

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:36.383
one of the F-14s had three cameras

00:09:36.383 --> 00:09:37.920
total mounted onto it.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:40.490
But it wasn't just Cruise going solo --

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:43.140
the rest of the cast
trained alongside him.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.770
Cruise and the rest of the cast

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:46.450
needed to actually fly

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:48.900
for an even better viewing experience.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:50.400
Despite the potential risks,

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:51.900
there are aspects of flying

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.992
one simply couldn't see
if they used green screen,

00:09:54.992 --> 00:09:57.498
like these distortions in the face.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.380
What's your favorite Tom Cruise stunt?

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.371
Let us know in the comments.

